1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,775
Remember Wren? Wren found a cave full of pentaja... and the money of a whole village stopped working.

2
00:00:08,125 --> 00:00:23,056
Wren is made up. But again and again in history, money has had a Wren. A stone wheel. A glass bead. A seashell. A silver coin. Each time, someone found a cheaper way to make more of it.

3
00:00:23,407 --> 00:00:26,223
This is: Every Wren in History.

4
00:00:28,172 --> 00:00:42,763
Our first stop is Yap, a small island in the western Pacific. Yap has no good stone for making money. So for generations, Yapese sailors crossed hundreds of kilometres of open ocean, to the islands of Palau.

5
00:00:43,113 --> 00:00:57,207
There, with tools made of shell, they carved limestone into great wheels with a hole in the middle. They called them rai. Then they hauled them home, on canoes and rafts. Not everyone made it back.

6
00:00:57,557 --> 00:01:14,475
Every rai cost a dangerous voyage, so there were never many. That's what made them precious: their size, the whiteness of the stone, and how few ever made it home. A stone about 2 feet across could buy a big pig, or 50 baskets of food.

7
00:01:14,825 --> 00:01:32,472
The biggest stones were far too heavy to move. So nobody moved them. When one changed hands, everyone just remembered who owned it now. One family's fortune was a stone at the bottom of the sea, lost in a storm generations earlier. The village still counted it.

8
00:01:32,822 --> 00:01:50,394
Then, in 1871, an Irish-American captain called David O'Keefe was shipwrecked on Yap. He came back with a sailing ship and iron tools, and made the islanders an offer: he would bring their stones home from Palau, in exchange for dried coconut.

9
00:01:50,745 --> 00:02:00,534
Stones that had been rare in 1840 became common. By 1929, there were more than 13,000 on the island. The biggest were 12 feet across.

10
00:02:00,885 --> 00:02:17,409
Sound familiar? It's Wren's cave, all over again. But this time, the islanders noticed. By later accounts, O'Keefe's stones, easy to get and suddenly everywhere, traded for less than the old ones. Yap had spotted its Wren.

11
00:02:17,758 --> 00:02:35,901
On Yap, everyone knew each stone's story, so new stones could be priced lower, and old savings stayed safe. In Ulm Hollow, a pentaja is a pentaja: Wren's cave made everyone's pentaja worth less. Yap's money had a memory. Most money doesn't.

